var n={clip_points_to_area:"<h2>Keep points inside the area</h2><p><strong>A spatial operation.</strong> It takes a layer of points and a study area, and passes on the points that are in the area.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>Every point is tested against the study area's outline, and the ones inside come out the other side. The ones outside are counted and reported, not passed on.</p><p>The test is the same one the coverage check and the map use. A point counted inside here is inside there, and a figure you quote from one agrees with the other.</p><h3>Why you need it</h3><p>A source gives you what its own request asked for, and almost every source is bounded by a <strong>rectangle</strong>: OpenStreetMap's query takes a bounding box, a cloud dataset is read by extent, a country export is clipped to one. A rectangle is not a jurisdiction. Draw North Kivu \u2014 long, curved, a province of the DRC \u2014 and its bounding box covers large parts of Uganda and Rwanda; a facility search inside it returned 961 places of which 526 stood in the neighbouring countries.</p><p>This widget is where that is decided, visibly, on the canvas. Before it existed the decision was made privately inside whichever widget happened to need it, which meant it could not be seen, changed, or recorded.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><p>1. Connect the study area to the <strong>area</strong> port and the point layer to the <strong>points</strong> port. Both are required: the question is about these points against that area. 2. Leave <strong>Keep points that sit exactly on the outline</strong> ticked unless you need strict containment. 3. Run. The step reports how many were inside, how many sat on the boundary and how many fell outside, and passes the kept points on to whatever you wire next.</p><p>If every point falls outside, the step refuses and says so with the counts \u2014 that usually means the area is not the one you meant, or the points are somewhere else entirely.</p><h3>The one setting</h3><p><strong>Keep points that sit exactly on the outline.</strong> On by default. A point on the border is in that jurisdiction's edge, and silently folding it into one side or the other answers a question nobody asked. Turn it off for strict containment \u2014 which is what the OGC predicate <code>sfWithin</code> means, and it gives a different count on exactly the data where it matters.</p><h3>Seeing what it set aside</h3><p><strong>Show the excluded points on a connected map.</strong> On by default. They are drawn in the colour the map already uses for points outside the area, and they are <strong>not</strong> passed to anything downstream \u2014 the next widget receives only what is in the area.</p><p>The count tells you how many were set aside. Seeing them tells you whether setting them aside was right: 526 facilities in a tight cluster over one border is a different fact from 526 scattered in every direction, and no number distinguishes those.</p><h3>What it records</h3><p>The receipt names the predicate the kept set answers to, whether boundary points were kept, and three counts: inside, on the boundary, outside. A reader can tell how many the source offered and how many this area accepted, which is the number a reviewer asks for.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><p>It does not change a point. Nothing is moved, snapped or rounded; a point either is in the area or is not.</p><p>It does not know why a point is outside. A facility across a border, a coordinate entered the wrong way round and a geocoding error all look the same to a membership test. The coverage check is the widget for asking what to do about them, with reasons recorded.</p><p>It does not clip polygons or rasters. Those have their own widgets.</p><h3>Worked examples that use it</h3><p>None yet. It was built on 2026-10-20, from a defect found in a real study area.</p>",deduplicate_points:"<h2>Deduplicate point records</h2><p><strong>A comparison.</strong> It takes two or more point layers from different sources, finds the records that are the same place, and merges the pairs that agree. The pairs it is unsure about it gives to you.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>Two sources over one study area draw the same hospital twice. A count of facilities is then wrong by however many of those there are, and no amount of care downstream fixes it, because nothing downstream knows which records were the same place.</p><p>This compares records <strong>across</strong> sources and asks two questions of every pair it considers:</p><ul><li><strong>Do the names agree?</strong> Either exactly, after normalising, or by similarity.</li><li><strong>Are they in the same place?</strong> Within a distance you set.</li></ul><p>Each question that answers yes is worth one point. A pair scoring two merges on its own. A pair scoring one <strong>waits for you</strong>, because the two questions disagree and that is a judgement rather than a calculation. A pair scoring none was never a candidate.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><p>Two or more <strong>point</strong> layers, each from a different source. One layer has nothing to compare with, so two is the floor rather than the shape; use <strong>Add point input</strong> for a third and a fourth.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it decides</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Name attribute</strong></td><td>Which attribute carries the name. <code>name</code> for most sources.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Name comparison</strong></td><td>*Exact* after normalising, or *Jaro\u2013Winkler similarity*.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Similarity at or above</strong></td><td>The similarity at which two names count as agreeing.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Compare pairs within</strong></td><td>Pairs further apart than this are never compared at all.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Count as the same place within</strong></td><td>The distance at which two records are in one place.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Merge automatically at a score of</strong></td><td>Two means both questions must agree.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ask me at a score of</strong></td><td>One means a single agreement brings the pair to you.</td></tr></tbody></table><h4>Choosing the distances</h4><p>These are the two numbers worth measuring rather than accepting. Sources digitise a facility differently \u2014 one marks the building, another the compound gate, a third the signpost on the road \u2014 so how far apart two records of one facility sit is a property of <strong>your</strong> sources. Run it once with a generous *compare pairs within*, read the distances on the pairs it offers you, and set *count as the same place within* from what you see.</p><h4>Deciding a pair</h4><p>Select the widget and choose <strong>Review pairs</strong>. The dialog shows one pair at a time, with:</p><ul><li><strong>Both records side by side</strong>, each under the name of the source it came from.</li><li><strong>Every attribute either record carries</strong>, lined up in one table, with the fields that agree</li></ul><p>marked apart from the fields that differ. This is usually what settles it: a second field agreeing, or a second field flatly disagreeing. A field missing on one side shows as a gap, because a missing value is not agreement.</p><ul><li><strong>How far apart</strong> the records are, <strong>how closely the names matched</strong>, and which of the two tests</li></ul><p>agreed.</p><p>Answer <strong>The same place</strong> or <strong>Different places</strong>, and say why. The reason is required: a judgement nobody can read six months later is not a record of one. It is kept with the decision, and the decision is pinned to the records as they were \u2014 <strong>edit either record and the judgement lapses</strong>, rather than a stale answer riding on changed data. A decision can be taken back from the same dialog.</p><h3>Reading what you get</h3><p>A point layer with one record per place. A merged record keeps the first source's values and carries a <code>mergedFrom</code> attribute naming every record that went into it. Records with no usable location pass through untouched \u2014 they were never candidates, and nothing has been said about them.</p><p>The receipt records the whole rule: the name method and its threshold, both distances, both score thresholds, how many pairs were compared, how many the blocking rule withheld, how many matched, how many are still waiting, and for every candidate its score, its distance and its name similarity. A figure that reports a deduplicated count can therefore say what produced it.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><ul><li><strong>It will not look inside one source.</strong> Comparison is across sources, so a layer's duplicates of</li></ul><p>itself are not found.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not read abbreviations.</strong> <code>HGR Goma</code> and <code>H\xF4pital G\xE9n\xE9ral de R\xE9f\xE9rence de Goma</code> are one</li></ul><p>facility and this will not say so: there is no nickname dictionary, no transliteration, no phonetic key and no learned model. That pair reaches you in the review band, which is where the judgement belongs.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not merge on a name alone</strong>, at the settings it ships with. Two clinics in one town can</li></ul><p>share a name; two unrelated facilities can stand twenty metres apart.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not decide for you.</strong> A pair in the review band stays two records until somebody</li></ul><p>answers. The gate is on the merge, not on the run.</p><ul><li><strong>It cannot tell you the thresholds are right.</strong> They are declarations about your data, and the</li></ul><p>only way to know is to look at the pairs it offers.</p><h3>Where the method comes from</h3><p>The contracts are those of <code>EPIAI RECORDLINK</code> \u2014 blocking before comparison, binary contributions rather than a blended score, three classes rather than merge-or-not, and a missing value that never agrees with another missing value. This widget adds the spatial comparison and the merge the thresholds licence.</p>",isochrone:`<h2>Network isochrone</h2><p><strong>A process widget.</strong> It draws the area you can reach on foot within a stated time, walking along real streets, from one or more sites.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>From each site it walks outward along the street network until the time runs out, then draws a band around every street it reached. The result is one region per site \u2014 not a circle, because streets are not straight and do not go everywhere.</p><p>A walk has three parts and this charges for all three: <strong>to</strong> the nearest junction, <strong>along</strong> the streets, and <strong>off</strong> the street at the end to reach a door.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Connect</th><th>What it needs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>your sites</td><td>the points to walk from \u2014 pumps, clinics, depots</td></tr><tr><td>a street network</td><td>from the <strong>Street network</strong> widget</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Out come polygons, one per site. Draw them on a Map, or count your records into them with <strong>Summarize points in polygons</strong>.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><p>1. <strong>Add it</strong> from the palette, under Catchments. 2. <strong>Connect your sites and a street network.</strong> 3. <strong>Set the budget.</strong> *Minutes* \xD7 *metres per minute* is how far someone can walk \u2014 5 minutes at 75 m/min is 375 m of street. 4. <strong>Set the final walk off the street</strong> \u2014 how far from a street someone can get to a door. The band is this wide where there is time left and narrower where there is not. 5. <strong>Set the snap limit</strong> \u2014 how far a site may be from the nearest street before the result says so. 6. <strong>Check the zone.</strong> Measuring happens in metres, so the UTM zone is part of the answer, and every measuring step in one workflow must agree on it. The run is refused by name if they disagree. 7. <strong>Run the workflow</strong>, then read the regions on a map and the alert in the Inspector.</p><h3>How the travel time is spent</h3><p>Shortest paths are Dijkstra over the network's directed edges and the metre lengths it supplies, including the partial length of a curved edge where the budget runs out part-way along it.</p><p>All three legs of the journey are charged against the same budget:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Leg</th><th>What it costs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>the straight walk from the site onto the network</td><td>distance to the snapped street, at walking speed</td></tr><tr><td>the streets</td><td>the shortest path, by directed edge and supplied length</td></tr><tr><td>the final walk off the street to a door</td><td>the band width where time allows it, narrower where it does not</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Charging the last leg rather than adding it is what stops a region overshooting the time it is drawn for. The off-street band is an approximation of coverage beside the streets, and holes inside it remain unless infill is enabled.</p><p>Two cases are marked rather than dropped, and the widget names both:</p><ul><li><strong>A site further from a street than the snap limit</strong> is routed anyway and marked off-network.</li><li><strong>A site whose walk onto the network alone spends the budget</strong> gets no catchment at all.</li></ul><p>Coverage can also be cut short by the graph itself: a region stops where the downloaded network stops, which is a property of the extent that was downloaded and not of the travel time.</p><h3>Worked examples that use it</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Example</th><th>What it does there</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>John Snow \xB7 walking catchments</strong></td><td>five-minute walking regions around the eight Soho pumps, drawn as a plot and as an interactive map, with the 1854 deaths counted into them</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>What it will not do</h3><ul><li><strong>It is not a service area or a routed travel time.</strong> It is an approximation of shape, drawn</li></ul><p>to be read. Nobody agreed to it and no router produced it.</p><ul><li><strong>It assumes the ground beside a street can be walked</strong>, at walking speed, in any direction.</li></ul><p>Near the edge of the budget that is where the region is most generous.</p><ul><li><strong>It does not drop a site that is far from the streets.</strong> Such a site is routed anyway, with</li></ul><p>the straight line to the nearest junction charged against its budget. Its region is marked, the widget glows red, and the Inspector names the leg. That straight line assumes you can cross whatever lies between, so the region reaches further than the ground allows.</p><ul><li><strong>A site whose walk to the street would spend the whole budget gets no region at all.</strong> It</li></ul><p>reaches nothing in the stated time, which is the answer. It is named in the alert, and the note on the result says the counts are over the sites that reached something.</p><h4>The direction it misleads</h4><p>All of the above push the same way: <strong>a poorly connected place looks better served than it is.</strong> Streets and footpaths are mapped unevenly, and informal settlements least of all. Read a region over sparsely mapped ground as optimistic, check the alert for sites routed off the network, and check how far each site had to go to reach a street.</p><h3>Checking it yourself</h3><p>The lab's <a href="https://digital-twin-ph.github.io/validation-lab/lab/index.html?path=19-snap-vs-pyproj.ipynb" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">snapping notebook</a> measures the displacement on the example data, and the corridor check rebuilds the region from the stated rule with Shapely.</p>`,map_output:"<h2>Map</h2><p><strong>An output widget.</strong> It draws what your workflow produced. It computes nothing, changes nothing and decides nothing \u2014 if a number looks wrong on the map, the step that produced it is where to look.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>A map is a stack of <strong>layers</strong>, drawn in order from the ground up:</p><p>1. a <strong>basemap</strong>, if you choose one \u2014 streets or terrain, under everything else 2. a <strong>study area</strong>, as the ground your results sit on 3. <strong>polygons</strong> \u2014 catchments, hulls, cells or bands 4. <strong>points</strong> \u2014 your records, sites, or anything with a location 5. a <strong>raster</strong> or a <strong>street network</strong>, where you have one</p><p>You connect what you want drawn. There is no setting that says what kind of map this is.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Connect</th><th>To draw</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a study area</td><td>the boundary your work is inside</td></tr><tr><td>a point layer</td><td>records with locations \u2014 up to eight separate layers</td></tr><tr><td>catchment polygons</td><td>areas reached, covered or grouped</td></tr><tr><td>a reasoning result</td><td>points coloured by the decision a ruleset reached</td></tr><tr><td>a coverage check</td><td>points marked inside, outside or missing a location</td></tr><tr><td>a raster</td><td>prepared or clipped cells</td></tr><tr><td>a street network</td><td>the streets themselves</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>How to use it</h3><p>1. <strong>Add the Map</strong> from the palette, under Outputs. 2. <strong>Connect your first layer.</strong> Drag from any step that produced something drawable onto the map's <strong>+ Add a layer</strong> port, or pick it from <strong>Add a layer</strong> in the Inspector. The map works out which port it belongs to. 3. <strong>Add more layers</strong> the same way. A second point source gets a layer of its own rather than replacing the first. 4. <strong>Choose how it is drawn.</strong> *Static plot* exports as an SVG with a scale bar and a north arrow. *Interactive map* can be panned and zoomed, and is the only one that can show a basemap. 5. <strong>Choose a basemap</strong> if the geography matters \u2014 streets or terrain under your results. Leaving it at *None* keeps the map working with no connection at all. 6. <strong>Title it</strong>, and write the source note. Both appear on the map and in its export. 7. <strong>Run the workflow.</strong> The map appears as a Results tab.</p><h4>Reading what you get</h4><ul><li>The <strong>key</strong> names every layer drawn.</li><li>The <strong>scale bar</strong> and <strong>north arrow</strong> appear on a static plot. An interactive map has no</li></ul><p>single scale, so it shows neither.</p><ul><li>The <strong>source note</strong> carries whatever you wrote, plus the licence of anything the map used.</li></ul><h4>Symbols and colour</h4><p>Two things about a point are shown at once, in two separate channels, so neither has to be read off the other.</p><ul><li><strong>Colour says which layer the point came from.</strong> Each point layer you connect gets its own hue</li></ul><p>from the categorical palette, so two searches of two different sources are told apart on the map and listed separately in the layer control, where either can be switched off.</p><ul><li><strong>Fill says where the point stands.</strong> Filled inside the study area, hollow outside it, grey when</li></ul><p>the record has no usable location \u2014 those are not drawn on the map and are listed instead.</p><ul><li><strong>Each layer also gets a mark</strong>: circle, square, triangle, star, cross or X. A mark is chosen per</li></ul><p>layer in this widget's settings, and the legend draws the same mark the map draws. Colour alone is not readable by every reader, and a figure printed in greyscale has no colour at all.</p><ul><li><strong>Every symbol carries a dark outline</strong>, so it reads over any basemap \u2014 a terrain basemap runs</li></ul><p>through the same colours the palette uses, and a hollow mark over one is hard to pick out. It is a neutral, not a seventh colour, and a checkbox turns it off for a pale ground or a printed figure.</p><ul><li><strong>Ordered values use an ordered ramp.</strong> Travel-time bands run light to dark through one hue,</li></ul><p>which is a ColorBrewer sequential scheme. A category palette on an ordered variable invites the reader to see difference where there is sequence.</p><p>The map's receipt records all of this \u2014 which channel carries which variable, the palette and the authority it comes from, and each layer's mark and colour with whether you chose it or the map derived it \u2014 so a figure in a publication can be attributed.</p><h3>Worked examples that use it</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Example</th><th>What the map shows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>John Snow \xB7 outbreak extent</strong></td><td>the judged outline over 250 death locations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>John Snow \xB7 Voronoi catchments</strong></td><td>one catchment per pump, with deaths overlaid</td></tr><tr><td><strong>John Snow \xB7 walking catchments</strong></td><td>travel-time bands, as a plot and as an interactive map</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Old Naledi \xB7 diagnostic access</strong></td><td>facilities coloured by the decision a ruleset reached</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Old Naledi \xB7 raster clipping</strong></td><td>a clipped raster inside its boundary</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Manila \xB7 coastal exposure</strong></td><td>facilities with their assigned projection site</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Geoprivacy</strong></td><td>the same records before and after being moved</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>What it will not do</h3><ul><li><strong>It cannot draw everything at once.</strong> A map with both a raster and polygons connected draws</li></ul><p>the raster. The layers stay connected; only one is drawn. A street network is no longer one of these cases: points, a study area and a street network wired to one map are all drawn together, on the interactive presentation.</p><ul><li><strong>The static plot does not draw a street network beside points.</strong> The streets are drawn in a</li></ul><p>frame of their own size, so a map carrying both draws the points in the plot and says where the streets are. Set the presentation to interactive to see all of it in one picture.</p><ul><li><strong>It cannot reorder layers, or choose their colours.</strong> Draw order is fixed, and a layer's hue</li></ul><p>comes from its position in the palette. The mark is the one part of the symbol you choose.</p><ul><li><strong>A basemap is not included in the SVG export.</strong> The tiles come from an outside service as</li></ul><p>you look at the map, so a figure you export shows your layers on a blank ground.</p><ul><li><strong>Choosing a basemap sends the area you are viewing to that service.</strong> Everything else about</li></ul><p>this map works offline. For maps of sensitive locations that is worth knowing before you switch it on.</p><ul><li><strong>It cannot tell you whether a map is honest.</strong> Emphasis, framing and colour all change how a</li></ul><p>map reads, and none of them changes a number. See the layers you connected, and say in the source note what the map is about.</p>",network_input:"<h2>Street network</h2><p><strong>An input widget.</strong> It supplies the streets that walking-distance work is measured along: a graph of points joined by segments, each with a length.</p><h3>Three ways in</h3><p>Choosing one shows that method's controls and hides the others, so the panel describes the path you are on.</p><p><strong>Upload a network file.</strong> A GraphML or JSON graph prepared elsewhere. It keeps its own extent.</p><p><strong>Download from OpenStreetMap.</strong> The study area's bounds go to overpass-api.de, which is asked to count what it would send before anything is sent, so an area past the limit is refused rather than transferred and discarded. One request at a time, 60 seconds minimum between them plus 30 seconds per megabyte received: the service is donated and the pause is the courtesy.</p><p><strong>Read an extract on this device.</strong> A published <code>.osm.pbf</code> \u2014 Geofabrik's country files, or any other OSM extract \u2014 chosen with the file picker. The file is read where it sits and is never copied into this browser's storage; the study area's bounding box is applied while the extract is decoded, so a country file yields one province's streets. Most countries have no province-level extract published, which is the point: the same file serves every province and every workflow after the first, and nothing is downloaded twice.</p><p>The travel mode belongs to all three. Walking keeps footways, paths and steps; driving keeps roads and honours vehicle one-ways. At the scale of a province the choice decides how much there is to read; at the scale of a district it decides what the question means. An acquired ADM1 or ADM0 study area defaults to driving, because nobody walks across a province.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>It gives the workflow a walkable street network, either downloaded from OpenStreetMap for your study area or uploaded from a file you prepared elsewhere.</p><p>A downloaded network keeps the ways people can walk on \u2014 residential streets, footways, paths, steps, tracks, service roads and the ordinary road classes \u2014 and leaves out anything tagged as closed to pedestrians. Each way becomes a run of segments between the points it was drawn with.</p><p><strong>Graph limit.</strong> The limit counts every point in the graph, including each bend a street was drawn with, not only the places two streets meet. A town can hold three points for every real intersection and a road following a coastline can hold seventeen, so the same limit allows very different amounts of street in different places. The download size allowed follows this number, so raising it raises what can be fetched.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><p>It takes an <code>area</code> \u2014 the study area that says where to download \u2014 and emits a <code>network</code>, which the isochrone, the junction reachability step, the corridor and the snapping step all accept. A map can draw one directly.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><p>1. Connect a <strong>Study area</strong>, then open the Street network node. 2. Leave <strong>Input method</strong> on *Download from OpenStreetMap*, or switch to upload if you have a file. 3. Press <strong>Check endpoint status</strong> if you want to know what the servers can do right now: it reports free slots and, when there are none, when the next one frees. 4. Press <strong>Download and save OSM network</strong>. This is a separate action \u2014 running the workflow does not start a download. 5. If the area is large or the streets dense, raise the graph limit and download again.</p><p>Downloads are paced: one at a time, with a pause afterwards that grows with the size of what you received. A busy server is waited for rather than treated as a failure. A network you have already downloaded is kept, so asking the same question again costs no request at all.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><p>It does not download while you run the workflow. Acquisition is always something you ask for.</p><p>An area too large for one request is asked for in several, automatically. The single request is tried first; if the service's own count says it will not fit, the panel says so \u2014 *&quot;Too much for one request \u2014 working out how many parts it takes&quot;* \u2014 and then works through the tiles, reporting each. The grid comes from that count, so a dense city is divided more finely than an empty district of the same size, and a sparse area is still one request. Tiles overlap by the download margin so a street crossing a seam keeps both ends, and parts of the grid that miss your study area are never requested at all. <strong>Stop</strong> ends the whole sequence, and the pause earned is charged once for the total rather than once per part.</p><p>Whatever route it took, the result is clipped to your <strong>study area polygon</strong>, not to the box around it. A junction outside the outline is not in the network unless it falls within your download margin, which is fetched on purpose so journeys near the border still work; a road is cut at the border rather than trailing beyond it. The widget records how many junctions and segments that removed.</p><p>It does not stop a download that is arriving. A request that never begins to answer is given three minutes, which is a limit on waiting, not on downloading. Once the first bytes arrive there is a download in progress and no clock ends it, however long it takes: press <strong>Stop</strong> when you want to give up, and the panel says how much had arrived and that none of it was kept. While it runs the panel shows how much has arrived and how fast, averaged over the last few seconds, so a transfer in trouble reads as a falling number rather than a frozen one. If it goes quiet for ninety seconds the panel says that too, with how much had arrived, and keeps waiting for you to decide.</p><p>It does not offer to hand-edit a large graph. The <strong>Edit bounded network JSON</strong> box is for the kind of network a person writes or fixes by hand \u2014 the Soho graph is about 28 KB. Above 20,000 nodes-plus-edges it is not offered, and the panel says so. Serialising a province into that box cost 41.8 MB of pretty-printed JSON and 59.7 MB again after escaping, rebuilt every time the panel redrew, which was enough to kill the browser tab while nothing was being done. The file input beside it stays, so a large graph can still be replaced by a small one, and nothing about the graph itself changes: it is held, drawn, run and exported at any size. The limit is declared as the cap <code>json-editor-elements</code>, queryable in the ontology like the rest.</p><p>It does not guess your area. Without a connected study area there is nothing to bound the request.</p><p>It downloads one travel mode at a time. Choose walking or driving before you download, because the two are different graphs and not two labels on one:</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Walking keeps</th><th>Driving keeps</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Footways, paths, steps, pedestrian ways</td><td>yes</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td>Motorways, trunk roads and their slip roads</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>Ordinary streets \u2014 residential, unclassified, tertiary to primary</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>One-way restrictions it obeys</td><td>the ones that apply on foot</td><td>vehicle one-ways, and roundabouts</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A saved graph keeps the mode it was downloaded for, and the widget says which. To change mode, change it here and download again: the new graph replaces the old one. A widget further down the workflow that asks for a mode this graph cannot carry refuses the run and says so, rather than reporting minutes for a journey the streets do not support.</p><p>Distances along it are distances on those streets, and a network downloaded today describes the streets today, not the streets of whatever period your data comes from.</p><p>Before anything large is downloaded, the widget asks the service <strong>how much is there</strong>. Overpass answers that question directly, and an area holding more street data than this widget can take is refused with the number \u2014 after one cheap request rather than after several large ones that fail. An area so large that the service will not even count it is refused the same way, because that is what a public instance says when an area is beyond what it will process.</p><p>A saved network is kept beside the workflow rather than inside it, in the larger store your browser offers, so the size of a graph is no longer limited by what a workflow may hold. If that store is unavailable \u2014 private browsing, for instance \u2014 the application says &quot;small store only&quot; when it saves, and a large network may then be refused.</p><p>A driving graph carries no speed limits, no junction delay and no congestion. Travel time over it is one speed applied to every street, which is the same model walking uses and a weaker one for a car.</p><h3>Worked examples that use it</h3><p><strong>John Snow network isochrones</strong>, in the 1854 Cholera Outbreak in London project, uses a prepared Soho network to draw what each pump could be reached from on foot.</p>",point_input:"<h2>Point data</h2><p><strong>An input widget.</strong> It brings located records into a workflow \u2014 one row per thing, each with a position and whatever attributes came with it.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>It holds a set of points and the values attached to them: cases, households, facilities, pumps, sample sites. Everything downstream that works on points starts here.</p><p>Records arrive three ways. <strong>Import a file</strong> \u2014 GeoJSON, or a CSV with longitude and latitude columns. <strong>Place pins</strong> by clicking a map, for a handful of locations you know. <strong>Connect a source</strong> that produces points, such as a facility search.</p><p>A record may have no position. That is kept rather than dropped, because a case with no recorded address is still a case, and steps that need geometry say how many they had to leave out instead of quietly working with fewer.</p><p><strong>Columns and their kinds.</strong> Each attribute has a declared kind \u2014 number, text, or a term from a list you give. The data quality panel profiles a column, shows which values do not match its kind, and offers a repair. A repair is recorded with the node and appears in the run evidence; it never overwrites what the file held.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><p>It has no input unless you connect a source. Its output is <code>points</code>, which nearly everything accepts: maps, tables, distance measurement, catchments, summaries, rates, case series and the geoprivacy steps.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><p>1. Add the node and open it in the Inspector. 2. Import a file, or switch to pins and click the map. 3. Check the column kinds. A column read as text when it holds numbers will not band or sum. 4. Open <strong>Data quality</strong> to see what is missing or malformed before you build on it. 5. Give the node a label that says what the records are, not where they came from.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><p>It does not clean data silently. Nothing is coerced, dropped or filled in; what cannot be read is reported and left for you to decide.</p><p>It does not join one set of records to another. Two sets that share no key stay two sets.</p><p>It does not know what a row means. Whether 250 rows are 250 people or 250 addresses carrying 489 events is something your labels and your weights have to say.</p><h3>Worked examples that use it</h3><p><strong>John Snow Voronoi catchments</strong> loads 250 death locations and eight pumps. <strong>Measles in Hagelloch, 1861</strong> loads a 188-case line list. <strong>Old Naledi diagnostic access</strong> loads a facility registry.</p>",snap_to_network:`<h2>Snap to network</h2><p><strong>A process widget.</strong> It moves each of your sites onto the nearest junction of a street network, so that steps which travel along streets have somewhere to start.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>A route has to begin on the street network. Your sites almost never sit exactly on a junction \u2014 a pump is in a yard, a clinic is set back from the road \u2014 so this widget finds the nearest junction to each one and puts it there.</p><p>It also records <strong>how far each site moved</strong>, which is the number to watch.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Connect</th><th>What it needs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>your sites</td><td>the points to move</td></tr><tr><td>a street network</td><td>from the <strong>Street network</strong> widget</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Out come the same sites, at junctions, each carrying the distance it travelled. Feed them to <strong>Reachable junctions</strong> or a walking-catchment step.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><p>1. <strong>Add it</strong> from the palette, under Catchments. 2. <strong>Connect your sites and a street network.</strong> 3. <strong>Set the snap limit</strong> \u2014 how far a site may be from the network before the result says so. 4. <strong>Run the workflow</strong>, then read the snap distances in the output or the Inspector.</p><h3>Worked examples that use it</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Example</th><th>What it does there</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>John Snow \xB7 walking catchments</strong></td><td>puts the eight pumps onto the Soho street network before travel times are computed</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>What it will not do</h3><ul><li><strong>It will not leave your data where it was.</strong> Every site comes out at a junction. The output</li></ul><p>is derived data, not observations any more, and the furthest any site moved is recorded in the run evidence.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not drop a site that is far from the network.</strong> A site past the snap limit is still</li></ul><p>moved to its nearest junction and is <strong>marked</strong>: its first leg crosses ground no street covers. The widget glows red on the canvas and the Inspector lists each one with its distance. The limit decides whether the result says so, not whether the site is in it.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not tell you the move was acceptable.</strong> If a site travelled 200 m to reach a</li></ul><p>junction, everything measured afterwards starts 200 m from where it should, and that error appears nowhere in the final numbers. Check the snap distance every time.</p><p>When sites are marked, you have three honest options: raise the limit, extend the street network where it stops short, or accept the list and say so in what you publish.</p><h3>Checking it yourself</h3><p>The lab's <a href="https://digital-twin-ph.github.io/validation-lab/lab/index.html?path=19-snap-vs-pyproj.ipynb" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">snapping notebook</a> checks the junction choice against pyproj and measures each displacement against a five-minute walking budget \u2014 one of the eight example pumps was decided by 0.43 m.</p>`,study_area:"<h2>Study area</h2><p><strong>An input widget.</strong> It defines where your work takes place: one shape, kept on this device, that every later step can be scoped to.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>A study area holds one piece of geography and makes it available to the rest of the workflow. You can draw it, or take it from a published administrative boundary.</p><p><strong>Drawn.</strong> A bounding box, a polygon you click out, or a circle around a point. Quick, and it is yours \u2014 nothing outside the workflow knows what it means.</p><p><strong>From a boundary source.</strong> Chosen by country and level from geoBoundaries: ADM1 is usually a province or region, ADM2 a district or city, and what each level means differs by country. The level is checked before anything is downloaded, so you see how many units there are and how detailed they are before committing. You then pick the units you want, and the shape you get is the one the source published, vertex for vertex.</p><p>The shape is stored in longitude, latitude order (CRS84). Applying it writes N3 facts you can read in the <strong>N3 &amp; evidence</strong> tab.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><p>It has no input. Its output is an <code>area</code>, which the map, the buffer, the raster window, the facility search, the point grid, the coverage check and the area measurement all accept.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><p>1. Add the node and open it in the Inspector. 2. Press <strong>Choose study area</strong>. Pan to where you are working. 3. Either draw \u2014 <strong>Bounding box</strong>, <strong>Polygon</strong> or <strong>Point and radius</strong> \u2014 or open <strong>Use an administrative boundary</strong>, enter a country, pick a level, press <strong>Check this level</strong>, and choose the units you need. 4. Press <strong>Apply study area</strong>. 5. Give it a label that says what it is. The label travels into the receipts.</p><p>Changing the shape later keeps the old one: the Inspector lists every extent you have kept and restores any of them, which records a new extent carrying the old shape rather than overwriting anything.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><p>It does not simplify a boundary you took from a source. A published polygon is a claim about where a jurisdiction is, and thinning it would quietly change that claim.</p><p>It does not fetch data by itself. It says *where*; a source widget says *what*.</p><p>A drawn shape is not a jurisdiction. If your result needs to be about a named place, take the boundary from a source so the receipt can say whose line it is.</p><p>Several selected units stay several shapes. They are not merged into one outline and never become a bounding box around the group.</p><h3>Worked examples that use it</h3><p><strong>Old Naledi \xB7 diagnostic access</strong> uses a drawn settlement boundary. <strong>Old Naledi \xB7 raster clipping</strong> scopes a population raster to the same shape. The four <strong>1854 Cholera Outbreak in London</strong> workflows each use their own Soho extent, which is why three of them differ.</p>",summarize_combined_area:"<h2>Combined area</h2><p>Takes areas and gives back one area, or takes polygons and gives back one polygons result. It combines whatever kind it is given, and hands back the same kind.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p><strong>Given two study areas</strong> \u2014 say two adjacent provinces \u2014 it carries both outlines through as the parts of one study area. That area can then be given to everything a study area feeds: the street network, the facility sources, the clip, the buffer.</p><p><strong>Given a polygons result</strong> \u2014 catchments, hulls, buffers \u2014 it dissolves the ones that overlap or touch into single polygons and leaves separate ones separate, in a metric projection you choose.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><table><thead><tr><th>port</th><th>what it takes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>polygons</code></td><td>a polygons result, to dissolve</td></tr><tr><td><code>area</code> and <code>area2</code></td><td>two study areas, to combine</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Every port is always there. What the widget *yields* follows what you wire: connect areas and the output is an area; connect polygons and it is a polygons result. Nothing to set.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><p><strong>To combine two study areas.</strong> Add the widget, then connect one study area to <code>area</code> and the other to <code>area2</code>. The output becomes an area the moment the first one is wired, so it can be given to a Street network, a facility source, a clip or a buffer exactly as a single study area would be. Name it if you want; unnamed, it takes the parts' names joined with a plus.</p><p><strong>To dissolve a polygons result.</strong> Connect it to <code>polygons</code> and set the metric zone and hemisphere for the projection the dissolve is computed in. Overlapping and touching polygons become one; separate ones stay separate, and the count of each is reported.</p><h3>What it assumes</h3><p><strong>That a touching border should not be dissolved when combining areas.</strong> Two provinces that share a border come through as two parts, not one welded outline. A published outline is an authority's record of where a boundary lies; two authorities describe a shared border differently, and welding them would assert a third line that neither drew while still citing both as the source. Every question this application asks of a study area \u2014 is this point inside, what does an extract clip to, what are the bounds \u2014 is answered identically by two parts. What they do not do is pretend the border is gone.</p><p><strong>That dissolving IS right for polygons.</strong> A catchment is this application's own construction, not somebody's record of where a line is, so two that overlap are one reachable region and are merged.</p><p><strong>That the first area's spatial reference is the combination's.</strong> Combining areas held in different projections is a question nobody has asked here yet, and guessing an answer would bury it.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><ul><li>It will not dissolve areas, for the reason above.</li><li>It will not combine an area with a polygons result. They are different kinds and the output</li></ul><p>would have to be one of them.</p><ul><li>It will not reproject. The metric projection on the polygons path is yours to set; the area path</li></ul><p>carries the first part's reference through unchanged.</p><h3>What the receipt says</h3><p>On the area path: <code>fw:AreaCombination</code> as the activity, <code>prov:used</code> for each part, <code>fw:partCount</code>, and <code>fw:ringCount</code> on the geometry \u2014 so a reader can see the parts were kept rather than merged.</p><h3>Checked against</h3><p>The polygons path is checked against shapely/GEOS in the validation lab (<code>12-combined-area</code>, agrees: combined area 701,662.61 m\xB2 against 701,662.61 m\xB2, zero symmetric difference). <strong>That check covers the polygons path only.</strong> The area path, added 2026-10-22, has no external check yet, and no Dive Deeper lesson \u2014 those are two different repositories and two different questions: the lab recomputes what this widget computed, and a lesson teaches what the method IS.</p>",summarize_hull:`<h2>Observed extent (convex hull)</h2><p><strong>A process widget.</strong> It takes points and gives back one polygon: the smallest convex outline that contains them, with the area it encloses.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>Stretch an elastic band around all your points and let it go. The shape it settles into is the convex hull \u2014 the outline of where your observations are, with no dents.</p><p>Along with the shape you get two measurements the rest of your workflow can read: the <strong>area</strong> it encloses, in square metres, and its <strong>span</strong>, the distance across its longest axis.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Connect</th><th>What it needs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a point layer</td><td>at least three points that have locations</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Out comes one polygon. Connect it to a Map to see it, a Table to read its numbers, or a Ruleset to make a judgement about its size.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><p>1. <strong>Add it</strong> from the palette, under Summaries. 2. <strong>Connect a point layer.</strong> Points without a location sit the step out, and the notes say how many took part. 3. <strong>Check the zone.</strong> The widget measures in metres, so it projects your points first, and the UTM zone it uses is part of the answer. The Inspector suggests the zone that fits your data and tells you what a different one would cost. 4. <strong>Run the workflow</strong>, then read the area on the output or draw the outline on a map.</p><h3>Worked examples that use it</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Example</th><th>What it does there</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>John Snow \xB7 outbreak extent</strong></td><td>outlines the 250 death locations, and a ruleset judges whether the outbreak extent is large enough to look at</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>What it will not do</h3><ul><li><strong>It will not follow the shape of your data.</strong> A hull is convex, and cases along a river or</li></ul><p>facilities along a coast are not. The outline fills in the space between them, so it claims ground where nothing was observed. If that matters, do not use a hull.</p><ul><li><strong>It is not a boundary.</strong> It is an outline of what was *seen* \u2014 not a service area, not a</li></ul><p>catchment, not anything anyone agreed to. Where your observation stopped is not where the thing you were observing stopped.</p><ul><li><strong>Three points in a line enclose nothing</strong>, and the widget refuses rather than returning an</li></ul><p>area of zero.</p><ul><li><strong>Watch the span against the area.</strong> A hull much longer than it is wide is close to a line</li></ul><p>and should be read as one. The widget says so in its notes when it happens.</p><h3>Checking it yourself</h3><p>The lab's <a href="https://digital-twin-ph.github.io/validation-lab/lab/index.html?path=10-hull-vs-shapely.ipynb" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">hull notebook</a> recomputes the outline and the area with Shapely and compares them to this widget's.</p>`};var i=n,l=e=>Object.hasOwn(i,e),p=e=>`https://github.com/digital-twin-ph/fieldwork/blob/main/docs/widgets/${e}.md`;function g(e,o){return l(e)?`<button class="text-button" id="open-manual" data-manual="${o(e)}">Manual for this widget</button>`:""}function w(e,o,a){let s=i[e];if(!s)return;let t=document.createElement("dialog");t.id="manual-dialog",t.setAttribute("aria-labelledby","manual-title"),t.innerHTML=`<div class="manual-head"><h2 id="manual-title">${a(o)}</h2><button class="button" id="manual-close">Close</button></div><article class="manual-body">${s}</article><p class="muted">This page is part of the manual carried in this build. Its source is <a href="${p(e)}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">docs/widgets/${a(e)}.md</a>, and the widget's ontology entry points at it with <code>fw:userManual</code>.</p>`,document.body.append(t);let r=()=>{t.close(),t.remove()};t.querySelector("#manual-close").onclick=r,t.addEventListener("cancel",h=>{h.preventDefault(),r()}),t.showModal()}export{i as a,l as b,p as c,g as d,w as e};
